<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFrameworks>$(MeToolkitMauiTargetFrameworks)</TargetFrameworks>
    <UseMaui>true</UseMaui>
    <SingleProject>true</SingleProject>
    <AssemblyName>Me.Toolkit.Maui.Sizing</AssemblyName>
    <RootNamespace>Me.Toolkit.Maui.Sizing</RootNamespace>
    <PackageId>Me.Toolkit.Maui.Sizing</PackageId>
    <Product>Me.Toolkit.Maui.Sizing</Product>
    <Description>Relative sizes for .NET MAUI: a percentage of the parent, the window, the display or another element, in XAML or code.
WidthRequest="{me:Relative 30}" is 30% of the parent's width, and stays that way as the window is resized or the phone rotated. Works on any double property - WidthRequest, HeightRequest, FontSize, Spacing - with Min and Max to clamp it.</Description>
    <PackageTags>maui xaml markup-extension responsive relative size percentage viewport</PackageTags>
    <PackageReleaseNotes>26.9.28 fixes Mac Catalyst and adds to what a relative size can do.
Fixed: on Mac Catalyst with the iPad idiom - MAUI's default - window- and display-relative sizes were wrong: 23% small against the display, and against the window 30% large at launch and 23% small after a resize, because MAUI reports the window in different units at those two times. The window is now measured from the native UIWindow, and the display converted with Apple's 0.77.
Changed: sizes are whole numbers by default (Round=Units). Round=Pixels rounds to whole physical pixels of the window's display; Round=None leaves the value as calculated.
Added: Breakpoints='600:50 1200:33' choose the percentage by window width, like CSS media queries, or by the reference's own width with BreakpointsBy=Reference, like container queries. Relative Margin and Padding, on every side or the Sides chosen. SafeArea=True with To=Window leaves out the notch, cutout and system bars. On Windows, To=Display measures the monitor the window is on. RelativeSizing attached properties let a Style carry a relative size, and ClearRelativeSize removes one.
Added: build-time checks. An analyzer in the package reads the app's XAML and warns, at the line, about MTKS001-005: {me:Relative} in a Style setter, a markup extension as its unnamed argument - which throws at startup - nesting inside another extension, To=Self measuring the dimension it sets, and invalid options.
Verified on Windows, an Android 16 phone, Mac Catalyst on macOS 15 and an iPhone XR on iOS 18.
26.9.27 stamps the net10.0 assembly with the package version. 26.9.26 shipped it as 1.0.0.0 - the publish workflow's test step rebuilt that slice without the version before packing - while the Android, iOS, Mac Catalyst and Windows assemblies were right. Nothing else changed.
First release, 26.9.26.
{me:Relative} and SetRelativeSize size a double property as a percentage of the parent (less its padding), the window, the main display, an element named with x:Reference, or the nearest ancestor of a type. Width, Height, Shorter and Longer pick the dimension; Min and Max clamp the result; Portrait and Landscape give a different percentage for each orientation of the window. To=Self keeps an aspect ratio, Offset gives calc(50% - 8), and RelativeSizing's attached properties let a Style carry a relative size. The value is recomputed whenever the reference changes size or the element moves in the tree.</PackageReleaseNotes>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      Microsoft.Maui.Controls, unlike the other libraries' Microsoft.Maui.Core: markup extensions,
      bindings, VisualElement and Window all live in Controls, and there is no sizing a XAML element
      without them. Same version as the demo app, and the same line as Core elsewhere.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Maui.Controls" Version="10.0.101" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Me.Toolkit.Maui.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The XAML analyzer ships inside this package, under analyzers/dotnet/cs, so every app that uses
    {me:Relative} gets its build-time checks without a second package. Built first, not referenced:
    nothing in this library calls it, and its Roslyn dependency must not become this package's.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Me.Toolkit.Maui.Sizing.Analyzers\Me.Toolkit.Maui.Sizing.Analyzers.csproj"
                      ReferenceOutputAssembly="false"
                      PrivateAssets="all"
                      SetTargetFramework="TargetFramework=netstandard2.0"
                      SkipGetTargetFrameworkProperties="true" />
    <None Include="..\Me.Toolkit.Maui.Sizing.Analyzers\bin\$(Configuration)\netstandard2.0\Me.Toolkit.Maui.Sizing.Analyzers.dll"
          Pack="true"
          PackagePath="analyzers/dotnet/cs"
          Visible="false" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
